t :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315241} {:answer/id #uuid "360ca319-c367-5456-95fc-0950b0f34989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315434} {:answer/id #uuid "d96dcda9-8117-5826-8af2-5a84d7d6521f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d96dcda9-8117-5826-8af2-5a84d7d6521f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316329} :answer-field/id #uuid "58339bb5-e525-5e23-8c9a-df4ecfa1365d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316751} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ae3d01a-f8f9-5f0a-a61b-0b8fbeb38fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315176} {:answer/id #uuid "1c0f7d2e-035d-55bb-81e2-bdb00cba4ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315282} {:answer/id #uuid "281e05ce-1a19-5a40-a758-f08bd21f5d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c0f7d2e-035d-55bb-81e2-bdb00cba4ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315282} :answer-field/id #uuid "b15c37ad-1987-5287-8c8f-8494de399fe3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ac49bd9-9719-507d-b361-709e747ff5e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-342724" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=1.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=1,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{2\\cdot 0} \\\\ 1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c67e515c-772b-57db-ad7e-bc3bafd270b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c67e515c-772b-57db-ad7e-bc3bafd270b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316240} :answer-field/id #uuid "e40c8ae7-36d2-5187-91d9-5e9873b26864" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8b7de5b-aac2-510e-98ee-6d02baa0c52b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343279" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+(\\frac{2}{x}+1){y}^{′}+(2-x)y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=3.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -(\\frac{2}{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=3$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}}{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =3e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{3e}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{3e}{{2}^{2}}{e}^{-2}=\\frac{3}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "304d5117-ee46-5c5d-afa7-ca920f029e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397619} {:answer/id #uuid "ae08826c-6c1c-5987-ad7e-864c4360f574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397620} {:answer/id #uuid "b83dbcc8-f9de-571f-b680-6fc8b19ba9d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "304d5117-ee46-5c5d-afa7-ca920f029e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397619} :answer-field/id #uuid "622cd65e-d7fb-587b-8c10-1defb970706b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397618} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ef66bf4-a759-5faa-bbe6-c8479e7aca1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4Q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397623} {:answer/id #uuid "07f88880-7512-5cb1-85f0-9acaa96748b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397624} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd7fc4f-0bb8-5d15-9606-f2346f18bdfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397625} {:answer/id #uuid "52543647-4678-5589-9673-644a893cea31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4Q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecd7fc4f-0bb8-5d15-9606-f2346f18bdfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397625} :answer-field/id #uuid "80ef2068-6b95-504c-8d87-420eff7181f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397622} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e8310ce-fbd3-5538-b766-45d5514898c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397628} {:answer/id #uuid "e082f656-48a6-5575-b482-8353f60d9a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397629} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0a044d-7c2c-5bb4-bfbf-18edf83e4c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397630} {:answer/id #uuid "b96d58ae-2f44-580e-81fa-71567a86ef0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b96d58ae-2f44-580e-81fa-71567a86ef0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397631} :answer-field/id #uuid "70866db8-d47c-5d65-8fab-fa76cb30f2a9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65f6d9f7-7688-5460-b686-2b9904fe578a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343289" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=-1.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=-1,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=4W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =4\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=-1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{4\\cdot 0} \\\\ -1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=-{e}^{4x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"}]}
